        heading: "Drainage, Walls, and Anchors",
        body: "Drainage measures are designed like any other engineered system: sized, located, and detailed. Interceptor trenches with perforated pipe and free-draining backfill cut across the slope to capture shallow groundwater; deeper horizontal drains - small-diameter pipes drilled nearly horizontally into the hillside - relieve pressure in the soil mass itself and visibly flow during wet weather, giving the owner confirmation they are working. Surface water is kept out of the slide area with regrading and lined channels, because every gallon that infiltrates at the head of the slide adds driving force. The engineer specifies outlets that discharge to stable ground or a storm system, never onto the slope below.\n\nWhere drainage alone cannot reach the required factor of safety, structural mitigation takes over. Drilled-shaft or soldier-pile walls are embedded below the failure surface so the stable ground holds the wall and the wall holds the sliding mass; tieback anchors extend from the wall into stable soil or rock behind the slide, each proof-tested to verify capacity. Soil nailing - grouted bars installed into the slope face with a reinforced facing - stabilizes shallower failures and steepened cuts. The engineer designs the structural system for the loads the stability analysis assigns, details corrosion protection for a decades-long service life in aggressive soils, and requires construction observation because anchor and nail capacity can only be verified during installation.",
