        body: "Structural repairs come before waterproofing, because a wall that is still moving will defeat any seal. Epoxy injection welds a structural crack back into a monolithic wall - the engineer specifies it where the crack is stable and strength restoration matters. Bowed block walls get engineered restraint: carbon-fiber reinforcement for moderate bowing, steel soldier channels bolted to the wall and anchored to the floor system above and the slab below for severe cases, or wall anchors - steel plates in stable soil outside connected through the wall - that can actually pull a bowed wall back toward plumb over time. Each is designed for the site's soil pressure, and the engineer requires the exterior cause - the water and pressure - to be addressed alongside, or the wall will bow again.\n\nThe drainage system is the pressure relief the wall needs. An interior perimeter drain - perforated pipe set in stone beside the footing, under the slab edge - intercepts water at the wall-footing joint and carries it to a sump basin with a primary pump, a battery-backed secondary pump, and an alarm; the engineer sizes the basin and pumps for the inflow the diagnosis found, not for a catalog default. Exterior waterproofing - excavating to the footing, applying a membrane, adding protection board and a new footing drain - is the more complete answer where the wall is accessible, because it stops water before it touches the wall. The two are sometimes combined on the worst walls. Every system gets a maintenance plan: pumps tested, drains flushed, and discharge points kept clear, because waterproofing that is never maintained eventually becomes archaeology.",
