        question: "What causes acid mine drainage in Appalachia?",
        answer: "When coal mining exposes pyrite (iron sulfide) in the rock to air and water, it oxidizes and produces sulfuric acid. That acid dissolves iron, aluminum, and manganese from the surrounding rock, creating the orange-stained, low-pH discharges common in Appalachian streams. Pre-regulation mines left thousands of these sources across Pennsylvania, West Virginia, Kentucky, and Ohio.",
