        heading: "Mortar Matching and Repointing Method",
        body: "Matching the original mortar is a materials science task. The engineer or preservation consultant analyzes the existing mortar--its binder (lime, natural cement, or early Portland blends), sand gradation, and any historic colorants--then specifies a replacement mix with equal or lower compressive strength and higher vapor permeability than the surrounding brick. Modern Type N or Type S mortar is almost never appropriate for 1800s Over-the-Rhine brick; a lime-rich mix is the default. Mock-up panels are built on an inconspicuous wall area so the color and tooling can be approved before the full facade is touched.\n\nExecution details decide whether the repair lasts. Joints are raked to a minimum depth of about twice the joint width, dust is flushed out, and mortar is packed in lifts rather than smeared across the face. Tooling matches the historic joint profile--typically a concave or weathered joint on Italianate facades. The work is scheduled for moderate temperatures and kept damp during curing, because lime mortar that dries too fast never develops its strength. The engineer specifies these means and methods in the documents rather than leaving them to field improvisation.",
