        body: "When wythes separate--the outer brick skin pulling away from the backup--helical stainless-steel ties are the standard repair. Installed from the interior through pilot holes, they stitch the wythes together without dismantling the facade, and the engineer lays out their spacing from the tributary wall area each tie must restrain. Where bulging is severe, the engineer may instead specify partial dismantling and rebuild of the affected area, salvaging sound brick and resetting it in matching mortar. Both approaches get verified against the wall's actual thickness and condition, never assumed from drawings that may not exist.\n\nLintels and parapets get their own engineering. Original timber or early steel lintels over windows and storefronts are inspected for section loss; where they have failed, the engineer designs replacement steel lintels sized for the masonry above, with proper bearing and flashing so the repair does not trap water. Parapets are checked for mortar loss and outward lean--an unbraced parapet with degraded mortar is a fall hazard--and the engineer either repoints and braces it or specifies a controlled takedown and rebuild. Storefront-level work is coordinated with the tenant and the historic board so structural repairs do not erase character-defining details.",
