        heading: "Adaptive Reuse Upgrades That Pay Back",
        body: "The upgrades that pay back fastest are the ones that unlock leasable space. Converting an upper floor to apartments typically needs floor strengthening, fire-rated separations, and new stairs or elevator shafts cut through existing structure--each opening engineered with headers, new supports, and shoring during construction. Ground-floor commercial space needs storefront lintel upgrades and often a new lateral element at the open front, where the original construction relied on the storefront itself for stiffness. The engineer sequences this work so the building stays stable at every stage, because removing a bearing wall before its replacement is in place is how partial collapses happen.\n\nLateral and diaphragm work is the invisible backbone. Many Huntington buildings have wood floors that act as flexible diaphragms with no reliable connection to the brick walls; the retrofit adds plywood overlays, steel collectors, and wall anchors that create a complete load path. New braced frames or shear walls go in where the lateral demand exceeds what the old masonry can deliver, detailed to yield in a ductile manner rather than failing brittlely. The engineer coordinates all of this with the MEP design, because new ductwork and sprinkler mains compete for the same ceiling space the strengthening steel needs.",
