        question: "Why do Appalachian slopes fail after heavy rain?",
        answer: "Most Appalachian hillsides are colluvial soil--loose, weathered material--sitting on bedrock. Dry, the soil has enough friction to stay put; saturated, water pressure between soil particles destroys that friction, and the whole layer can slide along the bedrock contact. Springs, poor drainage, and added water from development all raise that saturation, which is why stability analyses model the wet-season condition.",
