        question: "What floods shaped Johnstown's flood-control system?",
        answer: "Three catastrophic floods: 1889, when the South Fork Dam failed and killed over 2,200 people; 1936, the St. Patrick's Day flood that inundated the downtown; and 1977, when heavy rains overwhelmed the watershed again. Each disaster drove a new generation of flood-control construction--the channelization and dam projects that protect the city today were engineered directly from those events.",
