        body: "Floor vibration design starts with the floor's dynamic properties. The engineer targets a natural frequency and damping level that keeps footfall-induced vibration below human perception thresholds, using the industry's accepted design guides for residential floors. Longer spans--the open amenity and lounge areas student housing markets so heavily--need special attention: a 20-foot span that is strong enough can still feel bouncy, so the engineer adds mass, stiffness, or damping until the feel is right. Rooftop decks get the same treatment, because a amenity deck that vibrates under a crowd is a liability.\n\nDurability engineering anticipates how students actually treat buildings. Corridors and amenity spaces get structure that tolerates impact and heavy rolling loads; balcony railings and guards are designed for the code's concentrated loads with connections that cannot loosen under repeated use; exterior stairs and walkways are detailed for Morgantown's freeze-thaw cycles and de-icing salts. The engineer specifies corrosion protection for exposed steel, durable finishes at high-wear structure, and access for inspecting the elements--like podium drainage--that fail silently when nobody looks at them.",
